---
// Self-Learning Dictionary (#996): fix a misheard word in pasted text and it joins
// your dictionary, with a three-second Undo; a word check on the Mac decides where a
// learned word applies later (#3105, #3242). Every claim traces to
// help/self-learning-dictionary.md, which owns the detail.
import FeatureShell from '../../layouts/FeatureShell.astro';
import '../../styles/features/self-learning.css';
import FeatureHero from '../../components/features/FeatureHero.astro';
import FeatureFilm from '../../components/features/FeatureFilm.astro';
import DemoIcon from '../../components/features/DemoIcon.astro';
import ImportReassurance from '../../components/features/ImportReassurance.astro';
import DownloadClosing from '../../components/DownloadClosing.astro';
---

<FeatureShell
  path="/features/self-learning-dictionary/"
  title="Self-Learning Dictionary: EnviousWispr learns as you edit"
  description="When you fix a misheard word in text you just pasted, EnviousWispr notices and saves the spelling to your dictionary, with three seconds to undo. Free."
  ogImage="/images/og/self-learning-dictionary.jpg"
  ogImageAlt="EnviousWispr Self-Learning Dictionary: fix it once, it remembers"
>
  <FeatureHero
    kind="self-learning-dictionary"
    label="Self-Learning Dictionary"
    title={'Fix a misheard word in your text,<br><span class="accent-ink">and EnviousWispr picks it up.</span>'}
    copy="When EnviousWispr pastes something slightly off and you fix it, an on-device check sees that you made a correction. If it agrees, the proper spelling goes straight into your dictionary. As long as you have Dictionary turned on, your Mac checks later dictations to see if you meant that word in that spot."
  >
    <FeatureFilm kind="selflearn" />
  </FeatureHero>
  <section class="benefit wrap reverse">
    <div class="benefit-copy">
      <span class="eyebrow">Everyday editing</span>
      <h2>Just edit your text<br /><span class="accent-ink">the way you normally would.</span></h2>
      <p>Dictate something, paste it, and edit whatever is wrong. When EnviousWispr spots a correction, a small pill shows what it saved, such as Added “Saoirse” to Dictionary, or “Siobhan” updated for a word you already had, with an Undo button for three seconds. If you leave it alone, it stays. New words appear in Your Words, while existing words simply log the new mishearing.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="benefit-visual">
      <ImportReassurance
        wrap={false}
        items={[
          { title: 'Three seconds to undo', copy: 'If it caught something you did not intend, click Undo on the pill. That removes a freshly added word or reverts an updated one back to where it was.' },
          { title: 'Marked in your dictionary', copy: 'You can see what was picked up automatically at any time. Words added this way have a small sparkle icon in Your Words, and the Auto-learned filter lets you view just those.' },
          { title: 'Supported apps', copy: 'It watches for corrections in Mail, your web browser, terminal windows, and web-based apps like WhatsApp. Password fields are never watched.' },
        ]}
      />
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="benefit wrap">
    <div class="benefit-copy">
      <span class="eyebrow">Context checking</span>
      <h2>A learned word is never<br /><span class="accent-ink">swapped in blindly.</span></h2>
      <p>EnviousWispr will not automatically replace text on its own. When a later dictation turns up a mishearing you corrected earlier, a local check on your Mac looks at that spot to decide if your word makes sense there. Only approved spots change. This works across every dictation language, for both live dictation and Transcribe a File, provided Enable Dictionary is on.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="benefit-visual">
      <div class="sl-check" aria-label="Illustrative example of the word check">
        <div class="sl-check-row approved">
          <span class="sl-check-mark"><DemoIcon name="check" /></span>
          <p>The build runs on <mark>Tuist</mark> now.</p>
          <small>Changed</small>
        </div>
        <div class="sl-check-row kept">
          <span class="sl-check-mark">=</span>
          <p>Give the lid a <mark>twist</mark>.</p>
          <small>Left as you said it</small>
        </div>
        <span class="sl-check-note">Learned word: Tuist, heard as “twist”. Illustrative example.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="matching-story wrap">
    <h2>Your corrections stay<br /><span class="accent-ink">on your computer.</span></h2>
    <p>Detecting corrections and checking context happen entirely on your Mac, regardless of your polish settings. Envious Labs never receives the text being watched, the word you fixed, or the mishearing. If you choose cloud polish, your dictation, with any learned words in it, goes directly to your chosen provider under your personal key.</p>
    <small><a href="/help/self-learning-dictionary/">How the Self-Learning Dictionary works</a>, including which apps it watches and how to turn it off.</small>
  </section>
  <DownloadClosing title="Just fix words as you write and let EnviousWispr pick them up." />
</FeatureShell>
